From 092b9eb210a5cfc133f4c2021aa2ce9247e90987 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 13:56:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(shim): end the Windows stdin copy no client will ever
 close

Every container with attached stdio takes a further 30.0 s to be deleted after
its process has already exited. Measured on Windows 11, 2026-08-14, on the run
that first carried a container end to end (`ctr tasks start`, real stdio):

  12:47:08.080  reaped child process
  12:47:38.089  level=ERROR msg="failed to shutdown io after delete"
                error="io shutdown: context deadline exceeded"

30.009 s, with the container's output already on the console. Nothing is being
waited for that arrives.

This is not the create-failure stall fixed in the previous patch. That one was
a process that never started, whose streams the guest never attached to
anything; the fix was to stop granting those teardowns the full drain budget.
Here the process ran, exited, and drained: `Delete` legitimately keeps the full
budget, and it is a different call site (`service.go`'s `Delete`, not
`Create`'s error paths).

What does not end is the *stdin* half. forwardIO's shutdown waits for two
things (`internal/shim/task/io.go`): `ioDone`, the output copies draining, and
`stdinDone`, the stdin copy reaching EOF and delivering its in-band
`CloseWrite`. `ioDone` is fine -- the guest closes stdout/stderr when the
process exits, which is exactly what the create-failure case could not do, and
the host `Wait` handler had already blocked on it and returned. `stdinDone` is
not: on Windows the stdin copy is `io.CopyBuffer` reading a named-pipe
connection, and a named pipe ends only when a peer disconnects.

Neither peer does.

  - The shim's `stdinEOF` (called by `CloseIO`, and by teardown as a safety
    net) only closes a `closeRequested` channel. That channel is examined
    *between* copies and while re-dialing after a detach -- never while a read
    is in flight, which is where the goroutine actually is.

  - containerd's own client-side stdio never disconnects either. In
    `pkg/cio/io_windows.go`, `copyIO` accepts the stdin connection inside a
    goroutine and appends only the *listener* to the cio's closers, so
    `cio.Close()` closes the listener and leaves the connection open; the
    Windows cio sets no `cancel` func at all, so `cio.Cancel()` -- which
    `task.Delete` calls before issuing the RPC -- does nothing. The connection
    stays open, held by a goroutine blocked copying an `os.Stdin` that a
    console never EOFs.

The Unix build does not have this bug, and the asymmetry is the whole
explanation: there `cio`'s `cancel` closes the client's own O_WRONLY FIFO
handle, and the shim drops its matching reference in `stdinEOF`, so the FIFO
reaches a real EOF and the copy returns. On Windows there is no second
reference to drop and no client close to wait for. The wait can only expire.

So `stdinEOF` now disconnects the client, by closing the connection the copy is
reading (`stdinClient` in `io_copystreams_windows.go`). Deliberately the cause
and not the symptom: shortening the ceiling would still leave a stream that
never closes, and would still truncate the ceiling for the output drain, which
is a wait worth its full budget.

Closing it cannot truncate stdin. The copy reads in a tight loop, so it is
blocked in a read only when the pipe's buffer is empty -- every byte written
before EOF was requested has already been relayed to the guest. A byte racing
the close would be one written after `CloseIO`, which the client has by then
promised not to send. The in-band `CloseWrite` that follows is unchanged and
still ordered after every relayed byte.

Detach and re-attach are unchanged, which is the property most at risk here: a
client disconnecting *without* `CloseIO` is still a detach, still delivers no
EOF, and the goroutine still waits indefinitely for a new client on the same
pipe path. Only an explicit `stdinEOF` closes anything. The one new interaction
is the race where a redial completes just as `stdinEOF` cancels it: the fresh
connection is closed rather than read, since reading it would block on exactly
the connection nobody will ever end.

Plain-file stdin is left alone. A file reaches EOF by itself, and closing one
mid-copy could truncate a redirect still being read.

Alongside, `io.go` now says *which* of the two waits expired. They share one
deadline, so `context deadline exceeded` alone does not distinguish "the guest
never closed the output streams" from "the stdin copy never ended" -- the two
have nothing in common but the timeout, and telling them apart from a log line
is otherwise a VM run away. The next such stall reports `io shutdown: output
drain: ...` or `io shutdown: stdin drain: ...`. If this fix is right, the line
does not appear at all; if the analysis above is wrong about which half stalls,
the next run says so in one word.

`TestCopyStreamsStdinEOFWithClientAttached` covers it with no VM: a client that
writes and then holds its connection open forever, as containerd's does, and a
`stdinDone` that must close anyway. It fails before this change and runs in the
existing windows-latest unit-test job.

Not executed. The stall was measured on hardware; the mechanism is read from
the two sources above and the fix is compiled for windows/amd64, windows/arm64,
linux/amd64 and linux/arm64. No VM has been booted with it, and the new test
has not been run on Windows -- this machine cannot run Windows binaries.
